<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project>
  <!-- Phase 1715 — this file exists so the oracle project can differ from the
       repository's settings in exactly ONE respect, and inherit every other.

       `Directory.Build.props` resolution stops at the first one found walking
       up, so a file here with no import would take the oracle project out of
       the repository's settings ENTIRELY. That was the first shape tried and
       it is wrong: central package management and the `FSharp.Core` pin live
       in the root file, so without them the project resolved the SDK's own
       implicit `FSharp.Core` and the referencing test project reported NU1605,
       a package downgrade. One pin, in one place, is the property worth
       keeping; the explicit import below is how this file keeps it.

       The one respect that differs is FS0025 (an incomplete pattern match),
       which the root escalates to an ERROR repository wide. That escalation is
       right everywhere a human writes F# here — the vocabulary is other
       people's, closed DUs arrive by package raise, and a warning on a green
       build gets scrolled past.

       None of that reasoning applies to `BoundedFold.fs`. It is not written
       here: it is GENERATED by the F* extractor from `../BoundedFold.fst`, and
       `../check.ps1` byte-compares it against a fresh extraction on every run,
       so a hand edit is a change the proof leg refuses rather than one it
       absorbs. The incomplete matches it contains are the extractor's field
       projectors over types whose totality the PROVER established — which is a
       stronger guarantee than the compiler warning asks for, obtained a
       different way.

       So FS0025 is demoted back to a WARNING here: still printed, still read,
       just not fatal. `NoWarn` would make it invisible instead, and `NoWarn` is
       deliberately not used. Nothing hand-written compiles in this directory,
       and nothing should.

       The demotion is a CLEAR of `WarningsAsErrors` rather than an entry in
       `WarningsNotAsErrors`, and that is a measured choice rather than a
       stylistic one: the F# targets here do not act on `WarningsNotAsErrors`,
       so a project that set it built green incrementally and then failed the
       solution build with 54 errors — a setting that appears to work and does
       not is worse than no setting at all. `WarningsAsErrors` holds FS0025 and
       nothing else in this repository, so clearing it removes exactly the one
       escalation this directory needs removed. -->
  <Import Project="$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove('Directory.Build.props', '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../'))" />
  <PropertyGroup>
    <WarningsAsErrors></WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
